Subject: Keep glibc string block pointers as pointers, not long int
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

Fil-C drops a pointer's capability the moment it is cast to an
integer, so a pointer that crosses a call as a long int arrives dead:
the callee rebuilds a pointer from the integer and any load through it
stops the program with "filc safety error: cannot read pointer with
null object".  string/memcmp.c passes its block pointers to the static
alignment helpers that way, so memcmp of OP_T_THRES (16) bytes or more
traps whenever the helpers are not inlined first: with filcc -O0, and
with nanofil, which does not inline them.  filcc -O2 happens to inline
memcmp_common_alignment and memcmp_not_common_alignment, which is why
the release glibc worked.  This source already finds the first
differing byte in memcmp_bytes arithmetically instead of through
pointers rebuilt from long int, so the two helpers are the remaining
sites that cast.

string/wordcopy.c has the same pattern in all four _wordcopy_*
helpers, and sysdeps/generic/memcopy.h declares them, and its
WORD_COPY_* macros pass block pointers, as long int.  On x86_64 this
glibc has no caller of the wordcopy helpers left (memcpy and memmove
no longer use WORD_COPY_*), so nothing traps through them today, but
the declarations still force every pointer through an integer.

The pointers now travel as op_t pointers, and the integer arithmetic
that remains, the alignment tests and the word rounding, stays inside
one function on uintptr_t casts of them.  Passing uintptr_t itself
across the call traps just the same way, so the parameter types are
pointers.

The regression check is the units check of
nanofil/survey/glibc/regress.nix, which compiles both units with filcc
-O0 and runs nanofil/survey/glibc/regress/driver.c: unpatched it traps
(exit 133), patched it prints "all checks passed".

